	\title{Cramér-Rao Bound Optimization for Joint Beamforming and Mode Selection in RDARS-Assisted ISAC Systems}


	\author{Ahmad Reza Hassanshahi, Rouhollah Amiri, Fereidoon Behnia }

	\maketitle

	\begin{abstract}

Integrated Sensing and Communication (ISAC) is a foundation of 6G networks, demanding architectures that simultaneously enhance sensing accuracy and communication reliability. This paper presents a Reconfigurable Distributed Antenna and Reflecting Surface (RDARS) aided ISAC framework, where an RDARS overcomes the limitations of conventional passive Reconfigurable Intelligent Surfaces (RIS) and Distributed Antenna Systems (DAS). By enabling each element to dynamically operate in either \textit{reflection} or \textit{connection} mode, RDARS synergistically harnesses reflection gain, distribution gain, and an additional mode-selection gain. We investigate the joint optimization of transmit beamforming at the base station and dynamic mode selection at the RDARS to minimize the sensing performance metric, namely the Cram\'er-Rao Bound (CRB) for target localization, while guaranteeing a minimum required Signal-to-Interference-plus-Noise Ratio (SINR) for multiple communication users. To solve the resulting non-convex and mixed-integer problem, we develop an efficient iterative algorithm based on the Alternating Optimization (AO) framework, effectively leveraging Majorization-Minimization (MM) and Penalty methods. Comprehensive simulations validate the proposed design, demonstrating that the dynamic RDARS configuration achieves a superior trade-off between the Position Error Bound (PEB) and communication SINR, significantly outperforming benchmark passive RIS and DAS systems.
\end{abstract}
\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Integrated Sensing and Communication (ISAC),
Reconfigurable Distributed Antennas and Reflecting Surface
(RDARS), Reconfigurable Intelligent Surface (RIS), Signal-to-Interference-plus-Noise
Ratio (SINR), Cramér-Rao Bound (CRB), beamforming, mode selection.
\end{IEEEkeywords}
\section{Introduction} \label{sec:1}
\IEEEPARstart{T}{he} relentless advancement towards 6G networks is characterized by a strategic migration to higher frequency bands, such as mmWave and Terahertz. This transition unlocks a paradigm where radio signals transcend their traditional role, facilitating not only high-speed data transmission but also the acquisition of high-resolution information about the surrounding environment \cite{saad2020vision,letaief2019roadmap,tataria20216g,zhang2021perceptive}. This convergence is formalized in Integrated Sensing and Communication (ISAC), a key enabling technology that effectively optimizes spectral and hardware resources by consolidating sensing and communication functionalities \cite{liu2022integrated,meng2025integrated,zhang2025integrated2,liu2020joint,keskin2025fundamental,zhang2022enabling}. Emerging applications, including autonomous navigation, smart cities, and industrial automation, demand not merely connectivity but an elevated capacity for environmental perception and comprehension \cite{liu2022integrated}. ISAC is thus poised to be integral to 6G, providing the critical real-time environmental awareness required for these applications \cite{liu2022integrated}. Furthermore, architectures like the Distributed Antenna Systems (DAS) and its evolutions (e.g., cell-free massive MIMO), which distribute network elements to reduce access distance, can provide a foundational infrastructure conducive to the deployment of sophisticated ISAC frameworks, despite challenges related to power and complexity \cite{ammar2021user,elhoushy2021cell}.
\subsection{Related Works}
The design of ISAC systems fundamentally involves a trade-off between communication performance and sensing accuracy, often quantified by distinct metrics. On the communication side, common objectives are the maximization of the sum rate \cite{zhang2020capacity,ning2023unified} or the guaranteeing of a minimum Signal-to-Interference-plus-Noise Ratio (SINR) for each user \cite{wang2022noma,zhi2022power}. Conversely, sensing performance is frequently evaluated through the maximization of radar Signal-to-Clutter-plus-Noise Ratio \cite{yu2022ris,liu2022radar}, beamforming gain towards targets \cite{liu2018toward,zhang2025low}, or the minimization of the Cramér-Rao Bound (CRB) \cite{liu2021cramer,song2023intelligent}, which provides a lower bound on the variance of unbiased estimators for sensing parameters like target direction or distance. The system architecture plays a pivotal role in managing these trade-offs. While DAS and their evolutions offer a \textit{distribution gain}, they incur high power consumption and hardware costs \cite{ammar2021user,elhoushy2021cell}. Reconfigurable Intelligent Surface (RIS) has emerged as a low-cost alternative to reshape the wireless environment \cite{liu2021reconfigurable,wu2019towards}. Subsequent variants like Simultaneously Transmitting and Reflecting RIS (STAR-RIS) \cite{xu2021star,mu2021simultaneously} and Hybrid Relay-RIS (HR-RIS) \cite{zhang2023active,long2021active} have been developed to overcome inherent limitations such as limited coverage and the multiplicative fading effect. Recent research has extensively explored the integration of these surfaces into ISAC, focusing on joint beamforming design for both fully-passive RIS and active/hybrid variants to balance communication and sensing objectives \cite{liu2021cramer,song2023intelligent,yu2023active,zhu2023joint,zhong2023joint,liu2023snr,chen2023simultaneous,qian2023sensing}. To harness the benefits of both DAS and RIS, the novel Reconfigurable Distributed Antennas and Reflecting Surfaces (RDARS) architecture was proposed in \cite{ma2024reconfigurable}. The RDARS, whose elements operating in either connected or reflected mode, achieves a \textit{hybrid distribution and reflection gain}. After the preliminary work in \cite{ma2024reconfigurable}, RDARS has begun to explore its potential by designing an active/passive beamformer in MIMO communication systems \cite{wang2024joint,zhang2025rate} and investigating the trade-off between rate and SNR in an RDARS-aided ISAC system \cite{zhang2025integrated}.
However, a critical gap remains unaddressed: the integration of RDARS within an ISAC framework optimized for sensing accuracy via the CRB. To the best of our knowledge, no existing work has jointly optimized transmit beamforming and RDARS configuration to minimize the position CRB for target localization under stringent communication QoS constraints. While \cite{zhang2025integrated} studies RDARS-aided ISAC using sensing SINR, our work adopts the position-domain CRB as the localization metric. Recent closely related developments include an RDARS ISAC/localization demonstration, hybrid-RIS mode-selection designs, active-RIS CRB optimization, and tri-mode RDARS-ISAC waveform/mode switching \cite{wang2023demo,lin2025hybrid,zhang2026activecrb,ji2026modeswitch}. None of these works addresses the joint connected/reflected RDARS mode selection and transmit design for position-CRB minimization under multi-user SINR constraints. In the proposed formulation, the RDARS configuration changes both the communication channel and the geometry-dependent Fisher information contributed by the BS/RDARS-target paths. This localization-oriented coupling, rather than the use of standard AO/MM tools by itself, is the main technical distinction from prior RDARS-ISAC designs.
\subsection{Contributions}
\label{subsec:contributions}
This paper jointly optimizes transmit beamforming and dynamic mode selection in an RDARS-aided ISAC system to minimize the CRB while ensuring multi-user SINR. The NP-hard problem, involving variable coupling and unit-modulus/binary constraints, is addressed via an Alternating Optimization (AO) framework combining relaxation, successive approximation/MM, and penalty methods. Simulations confirm its superiority over passive RIS and DAS benchmarks in beampattern, geometry, and Position Error Bound (PEB)-SINR trade-off. The main contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
 \item Tractable SINR and CRB expressions as functions of active beamforming \(\mathbf{F}\), reflection coefficients \(\boldsymbol{\Phi}\), and mode selection \(\mathbf{A}\), enabling a concrete problem formulation.

 \item An efficient AO-based iterative algorithm decomposing the problem into sub-problems, with MM/successive approximation handling non-convexities and penalty terms enforcing SINR and mode-selection consistency.

 \item Extensive simulations evaluating beampatterns, geometry, and sensing-communication trade-off. The proposed RDARS outperforms passive RIS and DAS, achieving lower PEB under same SINR constraints by exploiting hybrid \textit{distribution} and \textit{reflection} gains.
\end{itemize}
\subsection{Outline}
The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section II presents the RDARS-assisted ISAC system model, including communication and radar channels, and defines the SINR and CRB metrics. Section III derives the CRB via the FIM, establishing the theoretical lower bound for localization accuracy. Section IV formulates the joint beamforming and mode selection problem and develops an AO-based solution integrating MM and penalty techniques. Section V provides simulation results, including beampatterns, trade-off analyses, geometric evaluations, and parametric studies, to validate the superiority of the proposed scheme over benchmarks in terms of PEB and SINR. Finally, Section VI concludes the paper.
\textit{Notations:} Scalars, vectors, and matrices are denoted by italic, bold lowercase, and bold uppercase letters, respectively. For a matrix $\mathbf{A}$, $(\cdot)^{T}$, $(\cdot)^{*}$, $(\cdot)^{H}$, and $\operatorname{tr}(\cdot)$ denote transpose, conjugate, conjugate-transpose, and trace; $\mathbf{A}(i,j)$ denotes its $(i,j)$-th entry, and $\|\mathbf{A}\|_{F}$ is the Frobenius norm. $\operatorname{diag}(\mathbf{a})$ forms a diagonal matrix from vector $\mathbf{a}$, $\mathrm{blkdiag}(\cdot)$ forms a block-diagonal matrix, and $\operatorname{vec}(\cdot)$ denotes vectorization. For a complex number, $|\cdot|$ is the modulus and $\|\cdot\|$ the Euclidean norm; $\Re\{\cdot\}$ and $\Im\{\cdot\}$ extract real and imaginary parts. $\mathbb{E}\{\cdot\}$ denotes statistical expectation, and $\mathcal{CN}(\boldsymbol{\mu}, \mathbf{\Sigma})$ represents a CSCG distribution. $\mathbb{R}^{a\times b}$ and $\mathbb{C}^{a\times b}$ denote the sets of $a\times b$ real and complex matrices, respectively. $\mathbf{I}$ and $\mathbf{O}$ are the identity and zero matrices, whose dimensions are clear from the context.
\section{Problem Formulation And System Model}\label{sec:2}
\subsection{Scenario Description}
We consider an ISAC system comprising a Base Station (BS), $K$ single-antenna users, a target and an RDARS panel, operating under MIMO-OFDM signaling with \(N_s\) subcarriers and the subcarrier spacing \(\Delta f\). We use centered frequency indices $n\in\mathcal{N}_s\triangleq\{-(N_s-1)/2,\ldots,(N_s-1)/2\}$; for even $N_s$, these are half-integer offsets with respect to the band center. The objective is to accurately sense and locate the target while ensuring user communications remain at a desirable level. The performance is evaluated using the communication SINR and the radar CRB metrics. A schematic of the scenario is shown in Fig. \ref{fig:System-Model}.
The BS, equipped with $M_t=M_r=M$ antennas arranged in a Uniform Linear Array (ULA), concurrently serves $K$ users and detects a point target. The RDARS consists of $N$ elements arranged in a Uniform Planar Array (UPA) configuration, each dynamically switchable between two modes: \emph{reflected mode} (passive phase shifting) and \emph{connected mode} (active transmission and reception). Only $a \ll N$ elements operate in connected mode for hardware efficiency. The binary diagonal matrix $\mathbf{A} = \mathrm{diag}\{a_1, \dots, a_N\}$ indicates the mode of each element ($a_i = 1$ for connected, $a_i = 0$ for reflected). The reflection coefficients are modeled by $\boldsymbol{\Phi} = \mathrm{diag}\{\phi_1, \dots, \phi_N\}$, where $\phi_i=|\phi_i|e^{j\mathrm{arg}(\phi_i)}$ with $\mathrm{arg}({\phi}_{i})\in[0,2\pi)$ and $|\phi_i| = 1$. The overall RDARS reflection effect is captured by $(\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{A})\boldsymbol{\Phi}$. In downlink, the BS and connected RDARS elements act as a distributed antenna system. The transmitted signal is
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{X}[n] = \scalebox{0.9}{$\begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{x}_1[n]  \mathbf{x}_2[n] \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{F}_1  \mathbf{F}_2 \end{bmatrix} \mathbf{s}[n] \triangleq \mathbf{F}\mathbf{s}[n]$}, \quad \scalebox{0.9}{$\forall n\in\mathcal{N}_s$}.
\label{eq:transmitted_signal}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{s}[n] \sim \mathcal{CN}(\mathbf{0}, \mathbf{I}_K)$ contains user data symbols, and $\mathbf{F}_1 \in \mathbb{C}^{M \times K}$ and $\mathbf{F}_2 \in \mathbb{C}^{a \times K}$ are beamforming matrices at the BS and RDARS connected elements, respectively, and we define $\mathbf{R}_{X1}$ and $\mathbf{R}_{X2}$ as follows: 
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{R}_{X1} = \scalebox{0.9}{$\mathbf{F}_1 \mathbf{F}_1^H, \quad \quad 
\mathbf{R}_{X2} = \mathbf{F}_2 \mathbf{F}_2^H$}.
\label{eq:R_x}
\end{equation}
\subsection{Communication Channel}
The communication channels in the considered system are defined as follows. Let $\mathbf{H}_{br} \in \mathbb{C}^{N \times M}$ represent the channel matrix between the BS and RDARS, $\mathbf{h}_{bu,k} \in \mathbb{C}^{M}$ denote the channel vector from the BS to the $k$-th user, and $\mathbf{h}_{ru,k} \in \mathbb{C}^{N}$ signify the channel vector from the RDARS to the $k$-th user. All channel components are characterized by a general Rician fading model. Specifically, the BS-RDARS channel $\mathbf{H}_{br}$ is expressed as
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{H}_{br} = \scalebox{0.9}{$\sqrt{\alpha_{br}}\left( \sqrt{\kappa} \, \mathbf{H}_{br}^{\text{LoS}} + \sqrt{1 - \kappa} \, \mathbf{H}_{br}^{\text{NLoS}} \right)$},
\label{eq:channel_model}
\end{equation}
\noindent where $\alpha_{br}$ is the path loss coefficient, $\kappa$ denotes the Rician factor, $\mathbf{H}_{br}^{\text{NLoS}}$ represents the non-line-of-sight (NLoS) component whose entries are independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) complex Gaussian random variables, i.e., $\mathbf{H}_{br}^{\text{NLoS}} (i,j) \sim \mathcal{CN}(0,1)$, and $\mathbf{H}_{br}^{\text{LoS}}$ is the deterministic line-of-sight (LoS) component.
Considering the described system model, the received signal at the $k$-th user can be formulated as:
\begin{align}
\mathbf{Y}_{u,k}[n] &= \scalebox{0.8}{$\displaystyle \left( \mathbf{h}_{bu,k}^{T} + \mathbf{h}_{ru,k}^{T} (\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{A}) \boldsymbol{\Phi} \mathbf{H}_{br} \right) \mathbf{x}_{1}[n] + \mathbf{h}_{ru,k}^{T} \mathbf{A}_{a} \mathbf{x}_{2}[n] + \mathbf{Z}_{C,k}[n]$} \nonumber 
&= \scalebox{0.95}{$\mathbf{h}_{k}^{T} \mathbf{F} \mathbf{s}[n] + \mathbf{Z}_{C,k}[n]$}, \label{eq:received_signal}
\end{align}
\noindent where $\mathbf{h}_{k} = \left[ \mathbf{h}_{bu,k}^{T} + \mathbf{h}_{ru,k}^{T} (\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{A}) \boldsymbol{\Phi} \mathbf{H}_{br}, \quad \mathbf{h}_{ru,k}^{T} \mathbf{A}_{a} \right]^{T}$ represents the composite communication channel from the BS and the RDARS's connected elements to the $k$-th user, and $\mathbf{Z}_{C,k}[n] \sim \mathcal{CN}(\mathbf{0}, \mathbf{\sigma}^2_k)$ is the additive white Gaussian noise (AWGN). It is important to highlight the introduction of an additional selection matrix $\mathbf{A}_{a} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times a}$ in \eqref{eq:received_signal}, which is employed to handle the dimensionality mismatch between the signal vectors $\mathbf{x}_1[n]$ and $\mathbf{x}_2[n]$. Specifically, $\mathbf{A}_{a}$ is constructed by extracting the $a$ columns from the master selection matrix $\mathbf{A}$ that correspond to the connected-mode elements. This relationship can be compactly expressed as $\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{A}_a \mathbf{A}_a^{\mathrm{T}}$.
Based on the received communication signal model \eqref{eq:received_signal}, the SINR for the $k$-th user can be formulated as:
\begin{equation}
\textbf{SINR}_{u,k} = \frac{ \left| \mathbf{h}_{k}^{\mathrm{T}} \mathbf{f}_k \right|^2 }{ \sum_{i \neq k}^{K} \left| \mathbf{h}_{k}^{\mathrm{T}} \mathbf{f}_i \right|^2 + \sigma_{k}^2 },
\label{eq:sinr_formula}
\end{equation}
\noindent where $\mathbf{f}_i$ denotes the $i$-th column of the composite beamforming matrix $\mathbf{F}$, for $i = 1, \ldots, K$, representing the beamforming vector dedicated to user $i$.
