\subsubsection{Update of $\mathbf A_a$}
With $\boldsymbol\phi$, $s_{k,i}$, and $\mathbf A$ fixed, the connected-element selection matrix is updated from
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
\text{(P13)}\quad \min_{\mathbf A_a}\;&\operatorname{CRB}(\mathbf A_a)+\frac{1}{2\rho_1}\sum_{k,i}|\mathbf h_k^T\mathbf f_i-s_{k,i}|^2\nonumber
&+\frac{1}{2\rho_2}\|\mathbf A-\mathbf A_a\mathbf A_a^T\|_F^2\nonumber
\text{s.t.}\;&\sum_{l=1}^N[\mathbf A_a]_{l,j}=1,\quad [\mathbf A_a]_{l,j}\in\{0,1\},\nonumber
&\mathbf A_a^T\mathbf A_a=\mathbf I_a.
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
The last constraint ensures that the connected elements are distinct. Using a first-order local model of the CRB and the quadratic penalties, the resulting linearized binary subproblem is a finite assignment problem over $a$ columns and $N$ candidate rows; it is solved under the distinctness constraint, and the new $\mathbf A_a$ is accepted only if the original fixed-penalty objective is non-increasing. This update explicitly preserves the dependence of $\mathbf G_2$, $\mathbf G_4$, and $\mathbf G_5$ on the physical connected-element locations.
Problem (P1) is therefore handled by a local AO/penalty algorithm for a non-convex mixed-integer problem; global optimality is not claimed.
\subsection{Convergence and Complexity Analysis}
For fixed penalty parameters $\rho_1$ and $\rho_2$, the accepted $\boldsymbol\phi$, $\mathbf A$, and $\mathbf A_a$ updates are non-increasing in the penalized objective in (P6). The transmit block is solved through an SDR/SCA step, and a recovered beamformer is retained only when it is feasible and does not increase the fixed-RDARS CRB. Hence, within a fixed-penalty stage, the accepted objective sequence is bounded below and converges in value. Because (P1) contains rank-one, unit-modulus, and binary constraints, neither a KKT point nor a global optimum of the original mixed-integer problem is guaranteed. The continuation $\rho_1,\rho_2\downarrow0$ progressively enforces the coupling equalities; monotonicity is claimed only within each fixed-penalty stage.
Let $d=M+a$ and $n_x=\mathcal O(Kd^2)$ denote the number of real scalar variables in the lifted transmit block. A generic interior-point solution of (P4) has conservative worst-case scaling $\mathcal O(I_{\rm IP}n_x^3)=\mathcal O(I_{\rm IP}K^3d^6)$, where $I_{\rm IP}$ is the number of Newton steps; rank-one recovery adds at most $\mathcal O(Kd^3)$ per randomization batch. The phase update requires the dominant eigenvalue of an $N\times N$ Hermitian matrix, with $\mathcal O(N^3)$ complexity using a dense eigensolver. Updating $s_{k,i}$ requires $K$ one-dimensional searches with $K$ interference terms, i.e., $\mathcal O(K^2\log(1/\epsilon))$. The $\mathbf A$ update requires sorting, $\mathcal O(N\log N)$, while the linearized $\mathbf A_a$ assignment is polynomial in $a$ and $N$ and is negligible for $a\ll N$ compared with the SDP step. Thus, the lifted transmit update dominates the worst-case per-iteration complexity.
