\subsection{Beampatterns}
This section presents the beampattern results for the BS and RDARS, shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:BS-Beampattern} and Fig.~\ref{fig:RDARS-Beampattern}. The scenario considers a target at \(60^\circ\) azimuth, two users at \(10^\circ\) and \(20^\circ\) and an RDARS at \(45^\circ\). The RDARS and target are located at a distance of 150 meters from BS. The RDARS phase have been adjusted in a way that four beam configurations are evaluated: reflection beam focused on target, on users, randomly generated and optimally designed. The figures show four distinct peaks near the users, RDARS and target.
At user's locations, the strongest peak corresponds to \textit{phase on user}, whereas the weakest corresponds to \textit{phase on target} case. Near the target location, however, this behavior is reversed. The \textit{optimal phase} produces an intermediate peak between these two extremes.
For performance comparison, the PEB is used as an accuracy metric that is equal to the square root of the CRB. Using the calculated FIM, the PEB is defined as follows:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{P} \triangleq \sqrt{\operatorname{tr}\bigl(\mathbf{J}_e^{-1}(\mathbf{p})\bigr)} = \sqrt{ \operatorname{CRB}}.
\label{PEB_eq}
\end{equation}
Directing the beam closer to the users increases the communication power and improves the SINR performance, albeit at the cost of reduced radar power, leading to higher PEB and less accurate sensing. Conversely, steering the beam toward the target reverses this trade-off. To facilitate comparison across configurations, the corresponding SINR and PEB values are illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:BS-Beampattern}. The \textit{optimal phase case} achieves a balanced PEB of 5.48~m and outperforms other configurations in both metrics. Notably, while the "phase on target" configuration yields a lower PEB (2.150~m), it fails to meet the desired communication quality, achieving only 21.8~dB SINR compared to 30.9~dB for the optimal design, thereby demonstrating the effectiveness of our framework in balancing sensing and communication under constraints. To provide further engineering insights, we examine several special cases: the SISO case reveals the optimal phase alignment maximizing reflection gain; the single-user case illustrates the distribution gain of connected RDARS elements; the pure LoS scenario highlights the sensitivity of localization accuracy to the BS--RDARS--target geometry; and the high-SNR regime highlights the pronounced mode-selection gain, as the selection of connected elements shapes the FIM eigenvalue structure. Collectively, these simplified cases clarify the roles of reflection, distribution, and mode-selection gains, and offer practical design guidelines.
\subsection{ISAC Trade-off Analysis}
Fig.~\ref{fig:peb_nusers} and Fig.~\ref{fig:peb_sinr_th} investigate the trade-off between sensing and communication by analyzing the effects of two key parameters on the PEB: the number of users and the minimum SINR threshold \(\overline{\gamma}_{k}\). If the number of users or SINR threshold grow, the communication power will increase.
As a result, the radar power will decrease. therefore, PEB will increase, which indicates a decrease in localization accuracy.
The proposed RDARS framework generalizes two existing architectures: Passive RIS and DAS. These correspond to special cases of RDARS where the number of connected elements is zero or equal to the total number of elements, respectively. Since RDARS inherently combines both reflection and connection capabilities, it offers greater design flexibility and degrees of freedom. Also, due to the reduction in the number of connected elements in RDARS-based systems compared to DAS architecture, the need for costly RF chains is significantly reduced, which represents a very good advantage of RDARS. Consequently, as clearly illustrated in the results, RDARS achieves significantly better performance and a substantially lower PEB compared to passive RIS and DAS.
