\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
Integrated sensing and communication (ISAC) has emerged as a key enabler for sixth-generation (6G) networks, yet its broadcast nature at the physical layer leaves transmissions vulnerable to malicious eavesdropping, particularly when physical blockages degrade direct links. Reconfigurable intelligent surface (RIS) offers a promising defense paradigm at the physical layer to address these issues, by reconstructing virtual line-of-sight links and dynamically reshaping the spatial propagation environment. In this article, we provide a comprehensive overview of RIS-assisted secure ISAC systems, covering its fundamentals, practical applications, and open future research directions. We first outline cooperative deployment topologies and basic electromagnetic operating principles. We then analyze the technical advantages of passive RIS integration in mitigating bottlenecks in resource competition across communication secrecy, sensing precision, and hardware sustainability. We also present representative applications, including urban vehicular networks, monitoring with unmanned aerial vehicle support, industrial Internet-of-thing, and privacy in smart homes. A case study quantifies gains in secrecy rate and spatial nulling capabilities achieved through joint active and passive beamforming under full blockage of direct links. Finally, we identify key open challenges and future research directions to enable practical, scalable, and intelligent deployment of RIS-assisted secure ISAC systems.
\end{abstract}
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:Introduction}
\IEEEPARstart{A}{s} a transformative paradigm for 6G wireless networks, integrated sensing and communication (ISAC) significantly improves spectral and hardware efficiency by unifying communication and radar sensing functionalities on a shared platform \cite{liu2022}. However, owing to the inherent broadcast nature of electromagnetic waves, transmit beams of ISAC systems at the physical layer are highly susceptible to interception while radiating energy into space for target detection. This dual exposure risk not only imperils data privacy of legitimate users but also exposes sensitive environmental topologies and target state information to malicious eavesdroppers.
To mitigate these vulnerabilities, existing physical layer security schemes for ISAC networks predominantly rely on active beamforming and artificial noise (AN) injection at the base station (BS) side. Representative work in this direction includes sensing-aided physical layer security designs that jointly optimize secrecy rate and sensing estimation accuracy via AN-based precoding \cite{Su2024}. Although such active precoding effectively balances secrecy rate and sensing accuracy under favorable line-of-sight (LoS) conditions, its defense capability relies heavily on a stable spatial and geometric propagation environment. In high-frequency bands and ultra-dense deployment scenarios, wireless signals are severely prone to blockage by buildings and dynamic obstacles. Once the direct link is blocked or suffers severe shadowing, conventional transmitter-side active countermeasures experience severe performance degradation due to the uncontrollable and hostile nature of non-line-of-sight (NLoS) channels. Consequently, overcoming physical channel blockage and maintaining topological stability in obstructed environments have become critical bottlenecks for deploying secure ISAC systems.
To tackle these fundamental limitations, reconfigurable intelligent surface (RIS) offers a promising physical layer defense paradigm by virtue of its programmable propagation manipulation and link reconstruction capabilities \cite{li2025}. Consisting of an array of passive reflecting elements, RIS can digitally adjust the phase, amplitude, and polarization of incident waves, thereby achieving dynamic control over the reflected wavefront. When the direct propagation path is blocked, RIS can bypass physical barriers to establish cascaded reflected propagation paths among the BS, legitimate users, and sensing targets under blockage conditions \cite{liz2022}.
Compared with conventional active relays that entail substantial hardware costs and self-interference, a typical RIS operates in a quasi-passive reflection mode without requiring active radio frequency (RF) chains or active signal amplification. This noise-free characteristic is of vital technical value for ISAC systems, as it prevents fragile radar sensing echoes from being submerged in additive background noise. More importantly, synergistically optimizing active beamforming at the BS alongside passive phase shifts at the RIS creates distinct spatial degrees of freedom. This synergy between active and passive designs enables coherent signal enhancement toward legitimate directions while synthesizing sharp spatial nulls toward eavesdroppers, and substantially alleviates resource competition among communication secrecy, sensing accuracy, and hardware sustainability. Representative joint optimization designs for such RIS-assisted secure ISAC have been validated to deliver notable radar signal‑to‑noise ratio (SNR) and secrecy rate gains via alternating optimization frameworks \cite{chu2024}.
Against this backdrop, this article provides a comprehensive overview of the fundamentals,applications, and future directions of RIS-assisted secure ISAC systems. The remainder of this article is organized as follows. Section \ref{sec:system} introduces the hardware principles and deployment topologies of RIS-assisted secure ISAC. Section \ref{sec:adv} analyzes the technical advantages of passive RIS integration and presents representative applications. Section \ref{sec:enab} details key enabling technologies, emphasizing cascaded channel estimation, joint active and passive beamforming, and sensing-driven dynamic handovers. Section \ref{sec:case} provides a numerical case study to validate the achievable secrecy rate and spatial nulling gains under direct link blockages. Finally, Section \ref{sec:chal} identifies open technical challenges and future research directions, followed by concluding remarks in Section \ref{sec:con}.
\section{RIS-Assisted Secure ISAC Systems}
\label{sec:system}
In this section, we first introduce the hardware structure and electromagnetic principles of RIS, followed by an overview of RIS-assisted secure ISAC architectures and representative deployment schemes for effective security coverage.
\subsection{RIS Hardware and Electromagnetic Principles}
The technical superiority of typical RIS stems from its high spatial degrees of freedom (DoF) in manipulating electromagnetic (EM) space.
Structurally, RIS is implemented as a metasurface array comprising a vast number of subwavelength programmable elements\cite{elmossallamy2020reconfigurable}. Each reflecting element typically consists of a metallic patch layer, a dielectric substrate, and a bottom fully reflective metallic ground plane. To achieve dynamic phase adjustability, low-power control components, such as positive‑intrinsic‑negative (PIN) diodes or varactor diodes, are integrated inside each element. Regulating the bias voltage of the control circuit changes the equivalent impedance characteristics of the element, and delivers precise control over the phase shift of incident signals. Throughout this article, we focus on quasi-passive reflection-type RIS. Unless specified otherwise, we assume ideal hardware conditions for conceptual illustration.
Unlike traditional active relays,  typical RIS adopts a quasi‑passive controlled reflection mechanism. This mechanism involves no complex RF chain processing. Consequently, it introduces no additive thermal noise and avoids self-interference, which is critical for extracting weak perception echoes in ISAC networks. By driving hundreds of reflecting elements with a specific phase shift mask, asymmetric deflection of EM waves or energy focusing can be realized to achieve precise channel control\cite{vaquero2026near}. This capability provides the foundational hardware substrate to bypass physical obstacles and overcome severe channel blockages at the physical layer.
\subsection{Architectures of RIS-Assisted Secure ISAC}
Fig.~\ref{fig:1} presents the architectures of RIS-assisted secure ISAC in the blocked environment. This unified secure cooperative architecture comprises a BS, the RIS, legitimate users, and sensing targets. Within this architecture, the RIS serves as a spatial channel scheduling center, tasked with reconfiguring volatile channels to sustain the reliable operation of the secure ISAC system.
The operational principle relies on real-time reconstruction of cascaded channels. When interference or physical obstacles disrupt the direct path between the BS and the target, the system activates the RIS-assisted relaying procedure. The ISAC signals transmitted from the BS deviate from their original propagation routes and impinge on the RIS surface after the system explicitly identifies the RIS spatial location through sensing. The RIS then adopts reflective phase vectors optimized in advance to build cascaded virtual LoS links that bypass physical obstructions. This mechanism effectively avoids blockage of the direct path and restores secure communication services in NLoS regions. Meanwhile, highly directional reflective beams concentrate EM energy toward legitimate directions and naturally isolate potential eavesdropping paths through spatial configuration. This cooperative framework shifts the security defense paradigm from active transmit adaptation at the BS toward proactive physical channel topology reshaping.
In the sensing dimension, the RIS leverages beam focusing to overcome the severe double-path loss that dampens echo signals over long distances. This extends the effective detection range of the system.Furthermore, the RIS provides extra spatial observation dimensions from diverse NLoS angles. Optimizing reflection phases improves target‑parameter estimation for range, velocity and azimuth, and can help maintain high‑resolution perception under partial target occlusion.
