\subsection{Deployment Schemes}
Flexible RIS deployment strategies further enhance the architecture to guarantee link robustness under complex physical environments.
From the perspective of spatial mobility, the architecture supports combined fixed and mobile deployment schemes. Deployment with fixed infrastructure places RISs on building facades, tunnel entrances, or street lamps. Leveraging the quasi‑static property of such channels, this scheme determines optimal reflection positions through offline planning. It delivers persistent security compensation for long‑term blind spots such as urban canyons. Its main technical advantage lies in low channel state information (CSI) acquisition overhead, which sustains stability of cascaded links with high energy efficiency. By contrast, mobile tracking deployment brings additional spatial degrees of freedom. Lightweight RIS units mounted on unmanned aerial vehicles (UAV) or ground mobile robots enable dynamic adjustment of reflection poses following target trajectories. Combined with intelligent scheduling algorithms including deep reinforcement learning, a mobile RIS adaptively optimizes its three‑dimensional spatial position. It dynamically builds secure virtual LoS links according to requirements in real time for targets within highly volatile environments.
From the perspective of network topology, the deployment paradigm falls into centralized and distributed configurations. Centralized deployment places large‑scale RIS arrays at key traffic hubs. It simplifies control protocols between the BS and RIS, and delivers broad secure coverage through high‑gain narrow beams. Nevertheless, the distributed strategy offers better defensive resilience against extreme shadow fading brought by dense obstructions. Deploying multiple RIS units of small and medium sizes throughout the environment builds a multipath reflective network. These distributed units jointly reconfigure propagation links from different spatial angles. The approach mitigates connection outage risks triggered by single‑point blockage. It also improves target‑tracking accuracy and realizes a security upgrade from point‑type coverage to domain‑wide protection.
This evolution includes two key transitions. It moves from static patching to dynamic adaptive relaying, and shifts from gain obtained from a single array to multipath spatial diversity. These advances greatly extend the security boundaries of ISAC networks. With flexible deployment resource allocation, this architecture supports various vertical scenarios. Representative cases include vehicle coordination for intelligent transportation, illicit target tracking for UAV defense, and precision asset monitoring within the industrial Internet-of-things (IIoT).
