\subsection{Secrecy and Sensing Synergetic Gains}
The core value of  RIS lies in the comprehensive ISAC gains derived from its spatial degrees of freedom. Unlike conventional base stations fixed at static site locations,  RIS features superior deployment flexibility and morphological customizability, such as flexible conformable skins or building facades. This allows it to embed deeply into traditional signal dead zones. When communication or detection links are severed by obstructions, real-time optimization of the RIS reflection vectors constructs controlled virtual LoS paths. Such link reconfiguration can restore physical connectivity and simultaneously recover secure transmission and target sensing, and can turn adverse blocking environments into programmable security barriers\cite{shen2019secrecy}.
Physically, this gain originates from precise reshaping of spatial EM fields. Phase coordination across hundreds of elements produces highly directional narrow beams. It weakens the correlation between legitimate and eavesdropping channels. With careful phase optimization, such spatial decoupling can isolate eavesdropping paths at the physical layer to approach high secrecy capacity \cite{luo2021reconfigurable}. Meanwhile, RIS‑assisted covert‑communication schemes against unauthorized detectors have been explored in \cite{li2025covert}. Furthermore, this energy focusing mechanism can partially compensate for severe loss over double propagation paths suffered by perception echoes over long distances. By supplying spatial observation metrics with multiple dimensions, the RIS mitigates degradation from geometric constraints brought by the absence of a direct LoS path. This enables high‑precision localization and environmental modeling under complex dynamic scenarios and comprehensively improves secure ISAC capabilities.
\subsection{Economic and Hardware Advantages}
For ultra‑dense wireless networks, cost effectiveness and ecological sustainability govern large‑scale commercialization. A typical RIS controls the EM environment without costly RF chains, power amplifiers, or digital baseband units. Unlike active relays that require high‑capacity backhaul and continuous power supply, a passive RIS brings no active thermal noise amplification or self‑interference\cite{huang2019reconfigurable}. This characteristic simplifies receiver design. It prevents weak target sensing echoes from being overwhelmed by background noise generated by active components. It lowers the hardware threshold for perception with high precision.
This characteristic of low power consumption makes the architecture a promising candidate for future green security solutions. Network operators can take advantage of the inherent deployment flexibility of RIS to mitigate coverage holes with minimal capital expenditures. This approach greatly cuts long‑term operational expenditures. Such a flexible provisioning model balances network evolution against economic sustainability. It delivers robust physical‑layer security and ubiquitous perception even within industrial environments constrained by limited energy or remote deployment conditions. Table~\ref{tab:isac_comparison}  provides a qualitative comparison that summarizes these performance and economic benefits across different ISAC paradigms.
