\subsubsection{Urban V2X Coverage} 
Under a fixed deployment paradigm, large centralized RIS arrays at major intersections deliver secure long-range coverage for vehicle-to-everything (V2X) communications. Smaller distributed units cover minor intersections and route signals around corners. As shown in Scenario A of Fig.~\ref{fig:use}, dense high-rise buildings within urban canyons often create severe NLoS blind spots. These blind spots block direct propagation between the BS and moving vehicles. Deploying RISs on building facades allows the system to dynamically build cascaded virtual LoS links that bypass structural obstacles when propagation conditions permit. Using vehicle poses obtained in real time from secure ISAC techniques, the system optimizes reflective phase masks. It maintains high secrecy rates and target detection accuracy despite complex building occlusions.
\subsubsection{UAV-Aided Monitoring}
Employing a mobile tracking strategy, lightweight RIS units mounted on UAVs take advantage of three‑dimensional spatial freedom to reconstruct directional virtual LoS links according to real‑time requirements within NLoS regions. As shown in Scenario B of Fig.~\ref{fig:use}, mountain ridges or forest canopies frequently break direct communications between emergency vehicles or helicopters and ground handheld terminals during search‑and‑rescue operations and emergency monitoring tasks at low altitudes. Drawing upon situational awareness obtained in real time, a mobile RIS tunes its reflection geometry along target trajectories. This approach mitigates illicit‑interception risks at the physical layer for sensitive monitoring data and maintains continuous tracking.
\subsubsection{Industrial IoT}
Combining fixed and distributed configurations in IIoT environments, small passive RIS elements mounted on workshop surfaces establish a reflective network with multiple propagation paths that turns physical barriers into programmable security walls. Illustrated in Scenario C of Fig.~\ref{fig:use}, modern smart factories contain dense metallic equipment and moving robotic arms that induce intense electromagnetic reflections and sudden link blockages. Passive RIS networks can bypass these physical barriers and reduce privacy‑leakage risks arising from factory topology, offering instruction transmission resistant to interception and high‑precision positioning for collaborative industrial robots.
\subsubsection{Smart Home Privacy}
Under a deployment paradigm that combines fixed and distributed layouts, tiny RIS units embedded within walls or furniture bypass indoor partitions to achieve coverage without blind zones. As shown in Scenario D of Fig.~\ref{fig:use}, indoor concrete walls and household appliances often block direct propagation between the gateway and smart devices or residents. For sensitive health and behavior monitoring tasks, obfuscation at the physical layer and directional beam enhancement protect perception echoes and private data against unauthorized eavesdropping during transmission toward the indoor gateway.
\section{Key Enabling Technologies}
\label{sec:enab}
Translating the physical gains of RIS into system-level security protection capabilities requires overcoming key technical bottlenecks, including accurate acquisition of physical environmental parameters, joint resource optimization, and dynamic defense assisted by sensing.
\subsection{Cascaded Channel Estimation}
Within NLoS scenarios with physical occlusions, acquisition of reliable CSI acts as the logical starting point to build security boundaries at the physical layer. Different from conventional active communication networks, the secure ISAC architecture relies on RIS‑assisted downlink encrypted information transmission. Meanwhile, it captures weak radar echo parameters from potential targets or eavesdroppers. Since a typical RIS has no active baseband processing capabilities, cascaded channel estimation meets two key physical challenges. These challenges involve high dimensionality and extremely low received SNR. Existing robust approaches for cascaded channel estimation mainly adopt dedicated pilot sequences from the BS and exploit RIS spatial sparsity to reduce training overhead, as summarized in \cite{zhou2022channel}. Beyond that, system security support demands extraction of physical parameters with high precision during the channel estimation stage. Representative parameters include range, angle, and Doppler frequency. By using spatial‑geometric topology information inherent within sensing echoes, the system can backward infer relative spatial positions for moving obstacles, legitimate users, and potential eavesdroppers. Such capability supports precise reconstruction of controllable virtual LoS links under complex blocked environments. This sensing‑driven channel estimation mechanism lays a foundation with high precision for subsequent spatial directional encryption and interference injection.
\subsection{Active-Passive Beamforming}
Joint active and passive beamforming is essential to balance communication secrecy and radar sensing accuracy. In an RIS‑assisted secure ISAC system, BS transmit signals must simultaneously satisfy user quality-of-service (QoS) constraints, target illumination requirements, and eavesdropper suppression goals. This formulation yields a complex multi‑objective optimization problem. By jointly coordinating the BS transmit precoding matrix and the RIS phase‑shift mask, the system precisely allocates electromagnetic energy across the spatial domain. For security, destructive phase interference creates deep spatial nulls at eavesdropper locations to degrade interception channels \cite{liu2023drl}. For sensing, the design minimizes detection errors to approach the Cramér‑Rao bound. Dynamic weighting factors adaptively balance these metrics based on operational priorities, e.g., favoring secrecy in sensitive areas or sensing accuracy in high‑mobility environments. Consequently, this cooperative design mitigates resource competition between communication and sensing while maximizing protection at the physical layer.
\subsection{Dynamic Tracking and Handover}
To counter dynamic channel blockages caused by moving environmental obstacles, traditional reactive handovers usually lead to instantaneous security link interruptions and defense failures, as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:predictive_handover}(a). The core technique of this architecture lies in a sensing-driven predictive adaptive scheduling mechanism, depicted in Fig.~\ref{fig:predictive_handover}(b). This technique leverages the radar sensing functionality of the ISAC system to extract key kinematic parameters, such as velocity vectors, azimuth angles, and Doppler frequencies, by analyzing the scattered echoes of dynamic obstacles. As presented in \cite{xia2024sensing}, building upon multi‑target‑tracking algorithms or long short-term memory networks, the BS can accurately reconstruct relative spatial‑geometric motion trajectories for obstacles and controlled virtual LoS links over an extrapolated time axis.
When the system predicts, based on a geometric collision model, that the current virtual LoS path will be blocked by a newly appearing moving obstacle within an extremely short time window, collaborative scheduling among multiple RISs or a proactive handover of the reflected beam is triggered before the actual link disruption occurs (Fig.~\ref{fig:predictive_handover}(b)). This mechanism can support seamless transitions under favorable conditions and substantially reduce privacy‑leakage and identity‑exposure risks stemming from transient‑link outages and frequent re‑connection signaling, achieving full-lifecycle adaptive defense under dynamic topology evolution.
