\section{Case Study}
\label{sec:case}
To evaluate the security and sensing performance of the RIS-assisted ISAC system under severe physical blockages, numerical simulations are conducted in a representative multi-user scenario. The RIS serves as a core reconfigurable node to establish cascaded virtual LoS links when the direct transmission paths between the BS and users or targets are completely blocked. The primary objective is to maximize the total secure communication rate while satisfying BS transmit power limits, QoS requirements for legitimate users, and radar echo power thresholds for target detection. The optimization variables, including the BS transmit precoding vectors, the artificial noise covariance matrix, and the passive phase shift matrix at the RIS, are highly coupled. To address the nonconvexity arising from these coupled variables and the unit-modulus rank-one phase constraints, an alternating optimization framework based on semidefinite relaxation  and successive convex approximation is utilized.
The BS is equipped with $N_t = 16$ transmit antennas and $N_r = 12$ receive antennas, serving $K = 3$ legitimate users along with one potential eavesdropper that also acts as the sensing target, positioned in close physical proximity to the user cluster. The RIS consists of an array of $M = 36$ reflecting elements with an inter-element spacing of $\lambda/2$, matching the antenna layout at the BS. The operating carrier frequency is set to $f_c = 30\text{ GHz}$, and the maximum transmit power of the BS is $P_{\text{max}} = 35\text{ dBm}$. All cascaded channels associated with the RIS follow a distance-dependent path loss model, with a reference path loss of $C_0 = -20\text{ dB}$ at a reference distance of $D_0 = 1\text{ m}$. The path loss exponents are configured as $2.0$ for BS-RIS links and $2.2$ for RIS-user links. The Rician factors are set to $0\text{ dB}$, $8\text{ dB}$, and $12\text{ dB}$ for the BS-RIS, RIS-user, and RIS-eavesdropper links, respectively. The background noise powers at the legitimate users, the eavesdropper, and the BS receiver are set to $-90\text{ dBm}$, $-85\text{ dBm}$, and $-100\text{ dBm}$, respectively.
As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:ris_elements_rate}, the total secure communication rate increases monotonically with the number of RIS reflecting elements across all configurations, confirming the spatial beamforming gains provided by a larger reflective aperture. The proposed joint optimization scheme consistently outperforms all baseline configurations across all array sizes. The performance disparities highlight the critical role of dynamic phase reconfigurability. Specifically, the benchmark enforcing identical RIS reflection coefficients for downlink communication and uplink sensing (RIS-UL/DL-identical) limits system performance, while adopting uncoordinated random phase shifts during uplink sensing (RIS-UL-random) fails to collect target echoes coherently. Furthermore, benchmarks disabling artificial noise injection (Without AN) or BS sensing illumination constraints (BS sensing removed) exhibit substantial rate drops, demonstrating that dedicated artificial noise and full dual-functional coordination are indispensable for safeguarding confidential information.
