\section{Open Challenges and Future Directions}
\label{sec:chal}
\subsection{Synchronization and Hardware Limits}
High‑precision time‑frequency synchronization lays the foundation for passive RIS‑assisted secure detection and confidential transmission. A typical RIS possesses no active RF chains or baseband processing units, so direct estimation for its own clock offsets and phase noise cannot be realized. Under dynamic scenarios with channel blockage, cascaded channel fading induced by loss over double propagation paths yields extremely low received SNR for synchronization pilots. This condition greatly hinders convergence of blind synchronization algorithms and raises residual errors. Moreover, manufacturing constraints and deployment over long durations inevitably bring hardware impairments to reflective elements. Such impairments cover phase quantization errors, amplitude‑phase nonlinear coupling, and bias drifts from thermal aging. Imperfections at the physical layer produce distortion for reflection beams, pointing deviations, or elevated sidelobe levels. In severe cases, such performance degradation reduces radar range and angular resolution. It may leak confidential signal energy toward unintended directions and create interception chances for potential eavesdroppers. Therefore, development of robust synchronization mechanisms under fully passive constraints, as well as flexible beamforming algorithms to adaptively compensate for hardware impairments, remains a critical bottleneck.
\subsection{Hidden Eavesdropper Defense}
Existing security mechanisms at the physical layer mostly assume eavesdroppers act as active non‑cooperative targets. The system can estimate their interception channels by capturing their transmitted signals or pilot sequences. However, potential interception devices within practical 6G vertical industries such as privacy‑preserving smart homes and industrial IoT often stay fully hidden and emit no electromagnetic signals. Under such extreme non‑cooperative scenarios, a secure ISAC system must fully depend on radar sensing functions to actively detect and identify abnormal physical entities in the environment. Complex NLoS conditions make weak echoes scattered by hidden eavesdroppers easy to mix with background clutter and reflections from moving obstacles. The passive RIS offers spatial degrees of freedom. It supports accurate target classification and recognition for behavioral intent amid channel blockages and severe interference over multiple propagation paths. The system also needs to dynamically implement beam nulling decisions at the physical layer based on these outputs. All these capabilities together form a demanding technical challenge for closing the loop of sensing‑driven security.
\subsection{AI-Driven Security Management}
Under the vision of ubiquitous wireless networking, highly dynamic physical topologies such as fast-varying channels within applications require responsiveness at millisecond level for RIS phase configurations and active beam orchestration. Traditional convex optimization methods including semidefinite relaxation and block coordinate descent involve matrix inversion over high dimensions. Their computational complexity rises exponentially with RIS element quantity. Such approaches cannot satisfy latency constraints for scheduling obtained in real time. Therefore, integration of artificial intelligence for autonomous security management forms an inevitable research trend. Nevertheless, deployment for deep learning or deep reinforcement learning approaches within secure ISAC systems brings distinctive challenges. The network needs neural architectures with strong generalization to address out‑of‑distribution problems triggered by abrupt channel blockages. Under dual performance constraints for maximized communication secrecy and sustained high‑precision radar tracking, Artificial intelligence (AI) algorithms face extremely large and complex action spaces. These spaces cover continuous reflection phases and active precoding matrices and may lead to severe training divergence. Accordingly, development of lightweight, explainable deep learning frameworks driven by physical models supports full‑lifecycle adaptive closed‑loop security orchestration. This direction constitutes a core avenue for future research.
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:con}
This article surveyed RIS-assisted secure ISAC from the perspectives of fundamentals, applications, and future directions. RIS provides a new means of controlling the wireless propagation environment, offering additional spatial degrees of freedom to enhance physical-layer security and sensing performance, especially when direct links are blocked or severely degraded. The case study demonstrated that joint active and passive beamforming can substantially improve secrecy performance and enable effective spatial nulling under fully blocked direct links. More importantly, RIS-assisted secure ISAC extends the role of RIS beyond link enhancement, toward security-aware and sensing-aware control of the electromagnetic environment. Realizing this vision, however, calls for progress in hardware-efficient RIS architectures, scalable optimization, real-time channel and environment acquisition, and intelligent reconfiguration under dynamic network conditions. Addressing these challenges will be critical to transforming RIS-assisted secure ISAC from an emerging research paradigm into a practical foundation for secure and resilient 6G networks.
